WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.679 --> 00:00:09.759
If you asked fans to name the best&nbsp;
comic book villains of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.759 --> 00:00:15.639
Doctor Victor Von Doom would definitely appear&nbsp;
near the top of the list. A long-time enemy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:20.139
of the Fantastic Four, Doom is one of&nbsp;
Marvel's greatest characters — someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.139 --> 00:00:24.759
who can present a threat not just to&nbsp;
the Four but to the entire multiverse.

00:00:24.759 --> 00:00:31.899
In the comics, Doom is a scientist, warlock,&nbsp;
politician, and a true leader among men. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.899 --> 00:00:37.299
iconic face and body armor hide one of the&nbsp;
most brilliant strategic minds in existence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.299 --> 00:00:43.359
and Doom has used his brains to take on and&nbsp;
win against god-like beings. Doom is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.359 --> 00:00:49.119
not a one-note "evil" guy but a deeply tragic&nbsp;
figure and someone who's extremely pragmatic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.119 --> 00:00:52.839
always having a good reason&nbsp;
for his most ruthless actions.

00:00:52.839 --> 00:00:59.019
Unfortunately, all this magnificent complexity&nbsp;
and high-minded villainy has failed to translate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.019 --> 00:01:05.619
to the big screen. In the 2000s-era "Fantastic&nbsp;
Four" movies, Doom is just some scientist guy who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.619 --> 00:01:11.319
randomly gets superpowers, tries to attack the&nbsp;
Four, and gets his butt kicked. We see another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.319 --> 00:01:17.499
version of Doom in the 2015 reboot, this time&nbsp;
reimagined as a computer scientist turned madman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.499 --> 00:01:22.719
who wants to kill off all of humanity after&nbsp;
going insane because of his interdimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.719 --> 00:01:29.859
travels. Even actor Toby Kebbell admitted his 2015&nbsp;
incarnation was a misfire, saying via CinemaBlend,

00:01:29.859 --> 00:01:33.459
"I truly believe Doom is an&nbsp;
awesome villain ... and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:37.633
kind of always just do something goofy&nbsp;
with it. It's a shame, it's a shame."

00:01:37.633 --> 00:01:40.165
"It's time… to end this."

00:01:40.539 --> 00:01:45.099
The role of the mysterious siren has been&nbsp;
around since the earliest days of cinema.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.099 --> 00:01:50.439
She's the woman with the exotic clothes&nbsp;
and accent who can send men wild with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.439 --> 00:01:54.579
single glance. She's the woman who&nbsp;
the hero and villain can spend the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.579 --> 00:01:59.919
entire movie fighting over. And one of the&nbsp;
oldest Hollywood sirens is Queen Cleopatra.

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:07.899
From Elizabeth Taylor in 1963's "Cleopatra" to&nbsp;
Monica Bellucci in 2002's "Asterix &amp; Obelix:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.899 --> 00:02:13.299
Mission Cleopatra," most movies depicting&nbsp;
the ancient queen like to focus on her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:17.799
overwhelming beauty. This includes the&nbsp;
fact that she was able to seduce not one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.799 --> 00:02:23.259
but two powerful Roman leaders — Julius&nbsp;
Caesar and Mark Antony. But the truth is:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.259 --> 00:02:28.479
claims of her beauty have also been greatly&nbsp;
exaggerated by Hollywood. Most depictions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.479 --> 00:02:33.339
of Cleopatra that survive on coins and&nbsp;
as sculptures depict her as a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.339 --> 00:02:38.559
ordinary-looking woman. The Greek historian&nbsp;
Plutarch put it rather bluntly when he wrote,

00:02:38.559 --> 00:02:40.479
"For her beauty, we are told,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.479 --> 00:02:45.879
was in itself not altogether incomparable,&nbsp;
nor such as to strike those who saw her."

00:02:46.493 --> 00:02:47.493
[tut tut tut] "Shhh."

00:02:48.219 --> 00:02:53.499
It's been speculated that Cleopatra was able&nbsp;
to charm so many men mainly due to her wit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.499 --> 00:02:59.019
and intelligence rather than her looks. But that&nbsp;
angle doesn't fit very well with a visual medium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.019 --> 00:03:04.633
like cinema, where it's much easier to show men&nbsp;
falling for a radiantly beautiful Cleopatra.

00:03:05.116 --> 00:03:08.739
Victor Frankenstein, the lead&nbsp;
character of Mary Shelley's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.739 --> 00:03:13.419
1818 horror novel "Frankenstein"&nbsp;
is a brilliant doctor obsessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.419 --> 00:03:17.739
with understanding and perfecting a way&nbsp;
to create life and bring back the dead.

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:23.439
In the course of his research, Dr. Frankenstein&nbsp;
builds a monster out of dead human body parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.439 --> 00:03:26.319
successfully reanimating&nbsp;
the multi-corpse structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.319 --> 00:03:31.119
and giving it human intelligence. This&nbsp;
new creation is Frankenstein's monster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.119 --> 00:03:36.699
and he's appeared in many movies over the&nbsp;
decades as a slow-moving, barely comprehensible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.699 --> 00:03:41.559
simple-minded creature. But that's not how&nbsp;
the monster appears in the original novel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.559 --> 00:03:47.619
In Shelley's story, the monster is extremely&nbsp;
agile, fast, powerful, and self-reliant.

00:03:47.619 --> 00:03:52.839
He's also highly intelligent, able to speak&nbsp;
multiple languages, and strikes a hard bargain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.839 --> 00:03:58.419
with Frankenstein to furnish him with a mate so he&nbsp;
can start a family. The monster from the novel is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.419 --> 00:04:03.999
not a literal superhero as in Aaron Eckhart's&nbsp;
"I, Frankenstein" but a cunning, articulate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.999 --> 00:04:09.159
yet deeply tragic figure who both loves and&nbsp;
hates his creator and his own existence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.159 --> 00:04:15.279
The closest thing to the book is 1994's "Mary&nbsp;
Shelley's Frankenstein." Robert De Niro plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.279 --> 00:04:20.739
a version of the monster that hews closely to the&nbsp;
novel. But he's distinctly more handsome than his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.739 --> 00:04:26.019
absolutely monstrous-looking book counterpart.&nbsp;
His intelligence is also said to be a result of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.187 --> 00:04:36.387
Hollywood has never had a lot of luck with&nbsp;
pirate characters before Captain Jack Sparrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.387 --> 00:04:42.687
came along. The only movie pirate with any&nbsp;
significant currency was Captain Nemo. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.687 --> 00:04:47.727
even calling the character a pirate does a&nbsp;
disservice to his backstory as laid out by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.727 --> 00:04:52.287
writer Jules Verne in "Twenty Thousand Leagues&nbsp;
Under the Sea" and "The Mysterious Island."

00:04:52.287 --> 00:04:58.587
In the books, Nemo is the son of an Indian king&nbsp;
who was forced to flee from his home after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.587 --> 00:05:04.107
British imperialist army attacked. A scientific&nbsp;
visionary, Nemo had his special submarine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.107 --> 00:05:09.147
the Nautilus, built from parts from all&nbsp;
over the world, and he took to roaming the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.147 --> 00:05:14.067
seven seas with his crew. All the while,&nbsp;
Nemo burned with a hatred for the British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.067 --> 00:05:19.287
Empire and dreamed of taking revenge against the&nbsp;
foreign interlopers who'd conquered his kingdom.

00:05:19.287 --> 00:05:24.207
Unfortunately, all that complex backstory&nbsp;
is thrown out the window for the big-screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.207 --> 00:05:30.327
adaptations of Captain Nemo. Most film versions of&nbsp;
the character don't even bother making him Indian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.327 --> 00:05:36.927
with actors like James Mason, Herbert Lom, Patrick&nbsp;
Stewart, and Michael Caine playing him in various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.927 --> 00:05:43.167
capacities. Naseeruddin Shah was the only Indian&nbsp;
actor to play Nemo in "The League of Extraordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.167 --> 00:05:47.661
Gentlemen," but the submarine Nautilus&nbsp;
got more attention than Nemo in that film.

00:05:47.661 --> 00:05:51.712
"You underestimate the Nautilus."

00:05:52.047 --> 00:05:56.967
It's no secret that Hollywood is in dire&nbsp;
need of compelling female action heroes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.967 --> 00:06:04.047
Aside from the stray Sarah Connor or Imperator&nbsp;
Furiosa, the action movie landscape is largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.047 --> 00:06:09.387
dominated by large men with bulging biceps&nbsp;
and more guns than they know what to do with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.387 --> 00:06:13.827
And yet Hollywood has been sitting on the&nbsp;
perfect female action star all this time.

00:06:13.827 --> 00:06:19.227
Lara Croft was the protagonist of a series&nbsp;
of action-adventure video games called "Tomb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.227 --> 00:06:24.627
Raider" that blew up in popularity in the late&nbsp;
'90s. As the name of the franchise suggests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.627 --> 00:06:30.207
Lara is an adventuring archeologist with&nbsp;
a penchant for raiding tombs. She's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.207 --> 00:06:34.287
highly skilled combatant, escape&nbsp;
artist, linguist, and detective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.287 --> 00:06:38.727
along with possessing any other skill the&nbsp;
story needs her to have to win the day.

00:06:38.727 --> 00:06:44.907
It can be easy to imagine Lara leading her own&nbsp;
movie franchise as a combination of James Bond and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.907 --> 00:06:50.127
Indiana Jones. Yet the two attempts that Hollywood&nbsp;
has made to turn the character into an action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.127 --> 00:06:56.907
movie star have given mixed results. The Angelina&nbsp;
Jolie version of Lara existed in a world too campy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.907 --> 00:07:03.087
to be taken seriously. Differently, the 2018&nbsp;
reboot starring Alicia Vikander erred too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.087 --> 00:07:08.067
to the other side, taking all the fun out of the&nbsp;
character in its desire to be "gritty." Meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.067 --> 00:07:14.010
that thus far, fans are still waiting for a movie&nbsp;
as good as the 2013 "Tomb Raider" video game.

00:07:14.727 --> 00:07:20.667
Few novels in history have enjoyed the widespread&nbsp;
popularity of the "Harry Potter" books. The series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.667 --> 00:07:25.887
was a publishing phenomenon before Hollywood&nbsp;
jumped on the bandwagon, churning out eight movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.887 --> 00:07:31.287
based on the novels depicting the journey of Harry&nbsp;
on his quest to defeat the dreaded Lord Voldemort.

00:07:31.287 --> 00:07:36.627
What made the franchise so beloved was the host&nbsp;
of fascinating and likable side characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.627 --> 00:07:42.747
most of whom translated well to the big screen.&nbsp;
But some weren't so lucky — like Ginny Weasley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.747 --> 00:07:47.307
who in the books is a fiery, funny,&nbsp;
intelligent character Harry finds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.307 --> 00:07:51.267
himself falling in love with. Fans of the&nbsp;
novels remember Ginny as someone who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.267 --> 00:07:55.827
unafraid to stand up to bullies and who&nbsp;
speaks her mind to everyone all the time.

00:07:55.827 --> 00:08:00.687
Unfortunately, the movies do a poor job&nbsp;
of reflecting those qualities in Ginny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.687 --> 00:08:05.427
played by Bonnie Wright. The character is a&nbsp;
pale shadow of her book self in the films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.427 --> 00:08:09.747
with hardly any important lines&nbsp;
or scenes to showcase her spunky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.747 --> 00:08:13.887
nature. Ginny's relationship with&nbsp;
Harry is also a lot more subdued,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.887 --> 00:08:19.518
to the point where you wonder why the two ever got&nbsp;
together given their noticeable lack of chemistry.

00:08:20.127 --> 00:08:25.707
If the Joker is the greatest villain from&nbsp;
Batman's rogues' gallery, Lex Luthor is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.707 --> 00:08:30.327
equally iconic archenemy to Superman. At&nbsp;
least, that's the way it shakes out in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.327 --> 00:08:35.847
comics. Despite being an ordinary mortal, the&nbsp;
comic version of Luthor actually feels like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.847 --> 00:08:40.707
credible threat to the Man of Steel in a way that&nbsp;
no other villain from the hero's mythology does.

00:08:40.707 --> 00:08:46.287
But the movies are a different story. Luthor is&nbsp;
frequently seen in live-action "Superman" movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.287 --> 00:08:51.747
but he rarely comes across as a foreboding&nbsp;
figure. The version of Luthor played by Gene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.747 --> 00:08:56.727
Hackman is way too campy to be threatening.&nbsp;
There's little evidence of the laser-focused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.727 --> 00:09:02.067
businessman or calculating genius from the comics&nbsp;
who's always three steps ahead of his enemies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.067 --> 00:09:07.107
Kevin Spacey could've brought those qualities&nbsp;
to the character in "Superman Returns," but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.107 --> 00:09:12.087
he instead chose to follow Hackman's lead&nbsp;
in making Luthor a hackneyed Bond villain.

00:09:12.087 --> 00:09:16.467
Then came Jesse Eisenberg's&nbsp;
take on Lex Luthor in the DCEU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.467 --> 00:09:20.787
and fans were left scratching their heads,&nbsp;
wondering what to make of the character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.787 --> 00:09:25.947
Eisenberg plays Luthor as a twitchy, nervy&nbsp;
millennial with a high-pitched voice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.947 --> 00:09:31.047
somehow managing to seem even less threatening&nbsp;
than Hackman's version of the character. So far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.047 --> 00:09:36.567
the closest thing to a live-action version of&nbsp;
Luthor from the comics was seen on the TV show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.567 --> 00:09:42.475
"Smallville," where Michael Rosenbaum managed&nbsp;
to add great depth and intelligence to the role.

00:09:42.987 --> 00:09:46.107
Batman is one of the most&nbsp;
popular superheroes in history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.107 --> 00:09:50.487
yet despite the Caped Crusader&nbsp;
being such a recognizable superhero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.487 --> 00:09:55.167
few of his supporting castmates from the&nbsp;
comics have been big hits on the big screen.

00:09:55.167 --> 00:10:00.387
The thing is, Batman has a good number of&nbsp;
superhero allies in the comics who rarely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.387 --> 00:10:06.207
get a mention in the movies. One of his most&nbsp;
important allies is Barbara Gordon, aka Batgirl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.207 --> 00:10:11.007
daughter of Commissioner James Gordon. Aside&nbsp;
from being a noble hero in her own right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.007 --> 00:10:15.867
Barbara has one of the most tragic character&nbsp;
arcs in the comics when she gets attacked by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.867 --> 00:10:20.247
the Joker and is permanently confined&nbsp;
to a wheelchair. Refusing to give up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.247 --> 00:10:24.447
Barbara finds a second life as&nbsp;
the cyber-hacker vigilante Oracle.

00:10:24.447 --> 00:10:28.467
Unfortunately, all this backstory has&nbsp;
never been shown on the big screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.467 --> 00:10:34.407
"Batman &amp; Robin" reimagined Barbara with Alicia&nbsp;
Silverstone as the niece of Batman's butler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.407 --> 00:10:39.807
who likes street racing and stumbles into becoming&nbsp;
Batgirl after having the costume handed to her.

00:10:39.807 --> 00:10:41.906
"Suit me up, uncle alfred."

00:10:42.267 --> 00:10:46.227
The Leslie Grace "Batgirl" movie was&nbsp;
canceled before it could ever see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.227 --> 00:10:50.547
light of day. And there doesn't seem to&nbsp;
be any plan to include Barbara in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.547 --> 00:10:56.012
two separate "Batman" franchises currently&nbsp;
in development under Matt Reeves and Todd&nbsp;&nbsp; Phillips.

